"# Default care\n\nBefore opening or updating a pull request: bring it up to date with the default branch and resolve any overlap yourself; read the whole diff once against the user's words and your self-brief, and revert or name every change the words did not ask for; update every document the change makes stale; and validate the change yourself, evidence first. Demonstrate the intent end-to-end: list the concrete actions an end user would take and their expected results; gather evidence for each: product-level artifacts that directly show the behavior working, such as screenshots, recordings, rendered UI, CLI transcripts, API responses, persisted state, or logs. Use as many artifacts as it takes; when the change has a visible surface, that includes pictures of it. Unit tests passing, generic pass/fail output, and reading the code are not evidence on their own. Keep every artifact you gather - pictures, recordings, transcripts, logs - in your task's evidence folder, never in the working copy or a commit; the description is written for a stranger, so no local path, private thread or personal detail enters it. Verified states what each artifact showed, or what stopped you gathering it. A picture the repository's own instructions keep with the change, such as a release note's hero picture, is part of the change, not evidence: commit it where they say. Show image artifacts on the merge card by their path in the evidence folder. Your self-brief also says whether a person will see the change and lists the views and states that will show it. Before your first edit, photograph each listed view that already exists and save the pictures in your evidence folder; a picture the captain attached, or one a reproduction produced, serves as a before picture. After the change, photograph every view or state a person would notice, as many as the change needs; photograph a view you shot before the same way - same window size, theme, data and state, cropped to the same region - and photograph again after any later edit or update that touches a view. Where the change is behavior a still cannot show - a drag, a keyboard journey, motion, progress, recovery from an error - your self-brief says so and why, and that view gets a demo recording in place of its pictures: the same action recorded before your first edit and after your last, at the same window size, theme, data and crop, through the project's own isolated test launcher, at real speed; a change whose point is how something looks keeps its pictures. Run the smallest set of existing tests that would catch a break in what an end user experiences, including end-to-end tests that cover the surfaces you changed, and re-run them after any rebase that brings in overlapping changes. Where the project's end-to-end tests read a surface through shared helpers, the tests that cover it are those helpers' callers, and a change that moves what a helper reads is fixed once in the helper, never in copies across the tests. If no existing test produces the evidence, write or improve a test so one does; if automated testing cannot, execute manual verification steps and record what you performed. During fixes, avoid unrequested rewrites of AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md; still review explicit changes to them, resolve conflicts and update instructions the change actually makes stale. Your report carries five headings in this order - Intent, What changed, Verified, Risk, Not done. Verified accounts for the complete validation - the exact commands and tests run, the manual checks performed, and the smallest evidence that the intent is met. Risk is low, medium or high with one sentence. A test whose only evidence is that source text contains or omits a string proves nothing; exercise an interface and assert behavior.\n"
